 [:br {}] "Highest Point: 1265 m" [:br {}] "Start Altitude: 1200 m" [:br {}] "Total Ascent: 65 m" [:br {}] "Total Descent: 65 m"]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "This was one of the three new ski mountaineering events, a sport which made its debut at Milano-Cortina 2026. The ski mountaineering events were held at the Stelvio Ski Centre in Bormio, Italy."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The men’s sprint event started at an altitude of 1,200 metres and ascended 65 metres over a track length of 725 metres before descending. Three heats were contested with six skiiers each, with the top three in each heat and the three fastest lucky losers advancing to the semi-finals. The top two in each semi-final and the next two fastest skiiers advanced to the final."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The favorite was Spain’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/156349"} "Oriol Cardona"] ", who was World Champion in 2023 and 2025, and had won a World Cup sprint earlier in the season. He was expected to be challenged by France’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/156501"} "Thibault Anselmet"] ", runner-up to Cardona at the 2023 and 2025 Worlds, but who had a win against him at the recent World Cup."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Both Cardona and Anselmet easily qualified for the final, joined there by Russian neutral athlete " [:a {:href "/athletes/140155"} "Nikita Filippov"] ", Swiss challengers " [:a {:href "/athletes/157092"} "Arno Lietha"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/157091"} "Jon Kistler"] ", who went 1-2 in the World Cup in Utah in early December, and Cardona’s teammate " [:a {:href "/athletes/140141"} "Ot Ferrer"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Cardona was mostly unchallenged in taking the gold medal, winning by over 1½ seconds over Filippov. Lietha was among the medalists for most of the race but was passed near the end by Anselmet who took the bronze medal."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "",